y  this  tricky sideline move queen to a3  so idea is very simple  black wants to keep pressurizing the d4  square  now when i check this position in online  database i found out that  at this position the highest played move  is  rook to b1  and let us understand why this is the  case  well it's a very tactical move by white  and the important point is black cannot  even touch the d4 pawn  for example if black takes with the  bishop then the simplest is white can  take this bishop and after knight takes  white can immediately wince black queen  after the move bishop to b4  so that's not a good choice for the  black  well what happens if you take with the  knight  well more or less same story here what  is going to capture your knight and  after black recapture  white has this lethal check  which i think more or less stemmed the  victory  for example if you play the move king to  d8 then after bishop to b4  once again you will lose your queen so  that is completely out of the box  if king to f8 then white has this  another lethal check bishop to h6  and now white is hitting the bishop  and if black foolish enough to play the  move bishop to g7  then he is going to lose his king  so last but not least what happens if  you play bishop to d7  well this time around white has this  sequence that is bishop captures  king captures  and now bishop to b4  and definitely this position is  disgusting for the black  so it is very clear that at this  position black cannot touch this d4 pawn  but white's idea is more nasty than this  let's consider a casual move by black  such as h6  well here comes the whole point  white wants to play the move d5  and when you move this knight let's say  to e5  then white wins immediately after the  move push up to p4  and now this black baby is completely  trapped  so you can understand now how dangerous  this rook to b1 move is  well considering all this before i move  on i like you to pause this video and  find out  an amazing move played by 